{{fbdoc item="title" value="Lua"}}----
Lightweight, embeddable scripting engine using the Lua language.

Website: https://www.lua.org
Platforms supported: ""Win32"", ""Win64"", Linux, FreeBSD, Haiku
Headers to include: Lua/lua.bi
Header version: 5.3.1
Examples: in examples/other-languages/Lua/

Haiku R1 beta 6 amd64: `examples/other-languages/Lua/minmax.bas` compiled and
ran with FreeBASIC 1.20.4-3 against HaikuPorts Lua 5.3, loading `minmax.lua`.

{{fbdoc item="section" value="Callback types and errors"}}
	Use a native Lua 5.3 SDK with these headers. The example's ##lua_Alloc## callback uses native-sized allocation counts, while a ##lua_CFunction## returns a C 32-bit ##Long## result count. Check ##lua_newstate## and the script result, and release the state with ##lua_close## after script execution.

	The min/max callback starts with the first converted number. Zero is a valid input, and an all-negative list must keep a negative maximum. Invalid or missing numeric arguments raise ordinary Lua argument errors which the script can catch with ##pcall##.

	On Win64, compile GAS64 callbacks with ##-eunwind## when a Lua error may leave the callback through native ##longjmp##. Windows needs unwind information for those frames. This does not run FreeBASIC destructors: the callback deliberately owns no local strings or objects requiring cleanup across an error.

	The native Win64 Lua 5.3.6 SDK has been checked against independent C layouts and both FreeBASIC backends. The original callback is exercised with positive, negative, zero and single-number lists, protected errors and missing-script cleanup, including its real final console keypress.

